  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Designs for Sport Beef Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      What is actually in the Designs for Health sports line's grass-fed beef protein, whether its NSF Certified for Sport listing holds up, and what each 25 g of protein costs next to Equip, Transparent Labs and Carnivor.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#what-is-it">Is It Beef Protein Isolate?</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in It?</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Is It Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#muscle">Is It as Good as Whey?</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#buyers">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#vs">vs Other Beef Proteins</a>
      <a href="#who">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Designs for Sport Beef Protein is a clean, NSF Certified for Sport, dairy-free powder with 21 g of protein per 27 g scoop, but it is one of the most expensive beef proteins per gram, at about $3.17 per 25 g. The protein is hydrolyzed bone broth collagen, which matters for muscle. Below: the label, the testing, the cost math and cheaper swaps.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Designs for Sport Beef Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Designs for Sport Beef Protein is a good product at a high price. Per the label at the time of writing, each 27 g scoop gives 21 g of protein and 100 to 110 calories, sweetened only with stevia, and NSF lists both the Chocolate and Vanilla flavors as Certified for Sport. At the $79.99 we saw on Amazon on November 20, 2026, it costs about $3.17 per 25 g of protein, roughly three times what Carnivor costs and about $1.15 more than Equip Prime or Transparent Labs.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you are a drug-tested athlete, cannot have dairy, and want a protein whose banned-substance screening you can look up in a public database. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you are buying protein mainly to build muscle on a budget: the protein is hydrolyzed bone broth collagen, which is lower in leucine than whey, and cheaper beef isolates on our <a href="/best-beef-protein-powder/">best beef protein powder</a> list cover the dairy-free need for much less.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers (Chocolate, per label)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">21 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per 27 g scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">100</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories per scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$3.17</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g at the Amazon price seen</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">NSF</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Certified for Sport (NSF database)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Designs for Sport Beef Protein and Four Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first card is the product under review. The next is its non-sport twin from Designs for Health, then three beef proteins that cost less per gram of protein. Prices are the Amazon prices we saw on November 20, 2026, and they change often.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="what-is-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is Designs for Sport Beef Protein Really Grass-Fed Beef Protein Isolate?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mostly yes, with one important nuance: the label calls the protein bone broth collagen protein, not muscle-meat beef isolate. The ingredient is HydroBEEF, a hydrolyzed beef protein from Swedish cattle that Designs for Health, the parent company, describes on its own product page as a collagen-rich beef protein isolate. Designs for Sport's technical sheet goes further and calls it a hydrolyzed bone broth protein that is high in the collagen-forming amino acids proline and glycine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Designs for Sport is a Designs for Health brand. The company's own press releases introduce it as "Designs for Sport, a Designs for Health brand", and retailers such as Vitacost list the product as "Designs For Health, Designs For Sport Grass-Fed Beef Protein". Designs for Health is a practitioner-focused supplement company, and Designs for Sport is the line it built for athletes, with most of its products carrying an NSF Certified for Sport listing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The grass-fed claim comes from the brand. Designs for Sport names the product Grass-Fed Beef Protein, its tech sheet lists "grass fed beef from Sweden" as a benefit, and the product page says the cattle are raised without hormones and fed a diet free of GMO grains, grasses and hay. We could not find an independent grass-fed certification, so treat it as a brand statement. The Swedish sourcing is consistent across Designs for Sport, Designs for Health and retailer listings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Why the distinction matters: beef protein powders come in two broad kinds. Some, like Carnivor, are made largely from beef muscle and connective tissue processed into an isolate. Others, like this one, are made from bone broth and hide-type proteins that are mostly collagen. Both are 90-plus percent protein by weight and both are dairy-free, but collagen-heavy protein has a different amino acid profile, which we cover in the muscle section below. Our <a href="/collagen-vs-protein-powder/">collagen vs protein powder</a> guide explains the difference in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a twin product. Designs for Health sells Bone Broth Protein, formerly called PurePaleo Protein, built on the same HydroBEEF ingredient with, per retailer listings, the same Chocolate ingredient list and the same 21 g of protein per 27 g scoop. The practical difference is the NSF Certified for Sport listing, which we found for the Designs for Sport tubs and not for the Designs for Health version. For a tested athlete that listing is the whole reason to pick the Sport label.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Designs for Sport Beef Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Designs for Sport Beef Protein has six ingredients, and protein makes up 21 g of each 27 g scoop. Per the Chocolate supplement-facts panel at the time of writing, one scoop has 100 calories, 1.5 g of fat, 3 g of carbohydrate including 1 g of fiber, 21 g of protein, 1 mg of iron, 140 mg of sodium and 150 mg of potassium. The Vanilla panel lists 110 calories, 1 g of fat, 4 g of carbohydrate, 21 g of protein, 150 mg of sodium and 75 mg of potassium.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per scoop (label)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Chocolate</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Vanilla</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Serving size</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">27 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">27 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">110</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.5 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbohydrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 g (1 g fiber)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sodium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">140 mg</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">150 mg</td>
