            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The Chocolate ingredient list reads: bone broth collagen protein (HydroBEEF), natural flavors, cocoa powder, medium chain triglycerides, steviol glycosides from organic stevia leaf, and silicon dioxide. Vanilla swaps cocoa powder for tapioca dextrin and lists the protein as bone broth protein (HydroBEEF). There is no added sugar, no sucralose or acesulfame potassium, no gums, no carrageenan and no artificial colors on either label.
          </p>

          <p>
            The minor ingredients each have a job. Medium chain triglycerides, a fat usually made from coconut or palm oil, help the powder disperse and give the shake some body. Silicon dioxide is a flow agent that stops clumping in the tub. Tapioca dextrin in Vanilla is a starch-derived carbohydrate used as a carrier, which is why Vanilla has one more gram of carbohydrate and 10 more calories than Chocolate. None of these is a concern at these amounts for most people.
          </p>

          <p>
            Designs for Sport says the powder is dairy-free, gluten-free, soy-free and non-GMO, and free of sucrose, fructose and artificial sweeteners. That makes it suitable for people with lactose intolerance or a milk allergy who would otherwise use whey. One caveat that rarely gets mentioned: people with alpha-gal syndrome, a tick-related allergy to red meat, can react to beef-derived products, so check with an allergist before using any beef protein if you have that diagnosis.
          </p>

          <p>
            The label numbers match what Designs for Health publishes for its twin Bone Broth Protein, which supports the idea that these are the same formula under two labels. Designs for Health states 21 g of protein for the flavored versions and 26 g for unflavored. Designs for Sport sells only the two flavored tubs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Designs for Sport Beef Protein Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes. Designs for Sport Beef Protein is NSF Certified for Sport: the NSF Certified for Sport database lists "Beef Protein Chocolate Flavor" and "Beef Protein Vanilla Flavor" under Designs for Sport, plus separate Canadian listings for both flavors. This is the strongest third-party claim a protein powder can make, and it is the main thing that separates this tub from cheaper beef proteins.
          </p>

          <p>
            NSF Certified for Sport is an independent program run by NSF. A certified product is checked to confirm that what is on the label is in the container, that it is screened for contaminants, and that it contains none of the more than 280 substances banned by major athletic organizations. The program also audits the manufacturing facility, and certified lots are tested on an ongoing basis. That is why professional leagues and many college programs ask their athletes to use NSF Certified for Sport products only.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the listing before you buy, because certifications are tied to specific products and sizes. Search the NSF Certified for Sport database for Designs for Sport and look for the Beef Protein entry matching your flavor. The product we reviewed is the 810 g (1.8 lb) tub, which is the only size Designs for Sport sells. Our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">best NSF certified protein powder</a> guide lists other powders whose certification we confirmed the same way.
          </p>

          <p>
            What NSF certification does not tell you: it is not a heavy metals score, a taste rating or a judgment on value. We did not find a published heavy metals result for this product from Clean Label Project, Consumer Reports or a similar tester, and Designs for Sport does not publish lot-level test results on its product page. Bone broth and other animal proteins can carry trace lead, so if heavy metals are your main concern, compare published results in our beef protein guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Of the five beef proteins in this review, Designs for Sport is the only one we found with an NSF Certified for Sport listing. Transparent Labs, Equip and MuscleMeds all say their products are third-party tested, but those are brand statements rather than a searchable certification. For a drug-tested athlete, that difference can be worth the extra dollar per serving.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="muscle" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Bone Broth Beef Protein as Good as Whey for Building Muscle?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No, gram for gram it is not, and that is the biggest weakness of Designs for Sport Beef Protein. Collagen-rich proteins like HydroBEEF are high in glycine, proline and hydroxyproline and relatively low in leucine, the amino acid most closely tied to switching on muscle protein synthesis. Whey is one of the richest common sources of leucine, which is why 21 g of bone broth protein does less for muscle than 21 g of whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            Designs for Sport says the product is a complete protein containing all nine essential amino acids, and its tech sheet cites a PDCAAS of 0.92 for beef protein against 1.0 for whey and casein. PDCAAS is a protein-quality score that caps out at 1.0. The brand does not publish a full amino acid profile or a leucine figure on its retail product page, so we cannot calculate leucine per scoop. Treat the 0.92 figure as the brand's claim.
          </p>

          <p>
            What that means in practice: if muscle gain is the goal and you have no dairy problem, whey isolate is cheaper and more effective per gram. If you cannot use dairy, a beef protein still works, but plan on a larger serving or pair it with protein-rich meals. The International Society of Sports Nutrition's position stand suggests about 0.25 g of protein per kilogram of body weight per meal and 1.4 to 2.0 g per kilogram per day for most exercising people; Designs for Sport's own tech sheet cites a range of 1.3 to 1.8 g per kilogram.
          </p>

          <p>
            The collagen content is not a waste. Glycine and proline are building blocks of connective tissue, and Designs for Sport positions the product for recovery and tissue support as well as lean mass. If joint and tendon health is part of why you train with a protein shake, collagen-rich beef protein is a reasonable fit. Just do not count every gram of it as equal to whey when you tally daily protein for muscle.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you tolerate whey but want a lighter, juice-style drink instead, a clear whey such as the one in our <a href="/isopure-clear-protein-powder-review/">Isopure Clear protein powder review</a> gives more leucine per gram. For dairy-free buyers, Carnivor and other muscle-meat beef isolates are the closer match to whey's profile, though they are less clean on sweeteners. Older adults, anyone with kidney disease, and pregnant or breastfeeding women should check protein targets with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a daily shake.
          </p>
        </div>

